### requests.http
### One block per act. Works with the IntelliJ HTTP Client / VS Code REST
### Client extensions -- each block is also given as the equivalent curl call
### in a comment, for the terminal.
###
### Seed data (data.sql), unchanged across every tag:
###   user 1: active, has a payment method on file
###   user 2: active, NO payment method on file
###   user 3: suspended

@host = http://localhost:8080

### Act 0/1: baseline GET, always 200.
# curl {{host}}/users/1
GET {{host}}/users/1

### Act 0: the hook. On step-0-baseline, before the agent's change, this
### upgrades user 2 fine (no payment check exists yet). After the agent adds
### the payment check live, this returns 500 -- no handler maps the new
### exception.
# curl -X POST {{host}}/users/2/upgrade -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"plan":"PRO"}'
POST {{host}}/users/2/upgrade
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "plan": "PRO"
}

### Act 2 onward (step-1-sealed+): the same three failure modes, now values.
### Upgrade succeeds for user 1.
# curl -X POST {{host}}/users/1/upgrade -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"plan":"PRO"}'
POST {{host}}/users/1/upgrade
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "plan": "PRO"
}

### ... is rejected (402 Payment Required) for user 2, no payment method.
# curl -i -X POST {{host}}/users/2/upgrade -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"plan":"PRO"}'
POST {{host}}/users/2/upgrade
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "plan": "PRO"
}

### ... is rejected (409 Conflict) for user 3, suspended.
# curl -i -X POST {{host}}/users/3/upgrade -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"plan":"PRO"}'
POST {{host}}/users/3/upgrade
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "plan": "PRO"
}

### ... and a GET for a user that doesn't exist is 404 Not Found.
# curl -i {{host}}/users/999
GET {{host}}/users/999

### Act 2, step 4: the key moment. Add `record Locked(long id) implements
### UpgradeResult {}` in UpgradeResult.java, then in the terminal:
# ./gradlew -q compileJava
### -> "the switch expression does not cover all possible input values"

### Act 5: the transaction gotcha. Run this against step-4-tx-bug (rejected
### upgrade, but the audit row still commits) and then against step-4-tx (it
### doesn't). Check the audit_log table via the H2 console to see the
### difference; AccountServiceTransactionTest asserts it directly too.
# curl -X POST {{host}}/users/3/upgrade -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"plan":"PRO"}'
POST {{host}}/users/3/upgrade
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "plan": "PRO"
}

### H2 console (spring.h2.console.enabled=true), JDBC URL from
### application.properties: jdbc:h2:mem:accounts
GET {{host}}/h2-console

### Act 6: the live demo prompt is the same on both branches:
###   "Accounts can now be locked after five failed logins. Reject upgrades
###    for locked accounts with HTTP 423."
### Run A against step-0-baseline (no skill), run B and C against
### step-5-agent (with skill). There's no fixed request here -- the new
### endpoint/field only exists once the agent (or you) adds it live.
